// React - 30-minute course-final MCQ (graded). Committed under the 2026-11-19 public-answer consent.
// 30 single-answer applied items, about 1 minute allotted per item (sizing only, no per-question timer),
// cumulative across all 26 React topics, ordered easy (q01-q08), medium (q09-q20) and hard (q21-q30).
// Distinct from the formative topic quizzes and from the MCQ part of the web-and-react module check.
// Exactly one correct answer per item, feedback on every option.

$CATEGORY: react/assessments/react-course-final

::react-cf30-q01::[html]<p>App renders <code>&lt;Score n\=\{4\} /&gt;</code>.</p><pre><code>function Score(\{ n \}) \{
&nbsp; return &lt;p title\=\{'Rated ' + n\}&gt;\{n * 2\} points&lt;/p&gt;;
\}</code></pre><p>What text shows, and what does the tooltip say?</p>{
~<code>n * 2</code> points, with the tooltip <code>'Rated ' + n</code>#Not quite. Braces evaluate JavaScript, in an attribute just as in text.
~4 points, with the tooltip Rated 4#Not quite. The text is the value of n * 2, which is 8.
=8 points, with the tooltip Rated 4#Correct. Each pair of braces evaluates its expression\: the title is 'Rated ' + 4, and the text is 4 * 2.
~8 points, with the tooltip Rated n#Not quite. Inside the braces, n is the variable, so the title reads Rated 4.
}

::react-cf30-q02::[html]<p>In a Vite project, Kai renames <code>src/App.jsx</code> to <code>src/Shell.jsx</code> and the component inside to Shell. The dev server now reports that it cannot resolve the import <code>./App.jsx</code>.</p><p>Which file still needs to change?</p>{
=<code>src/main.jsx</code>, which imports App and renders it into the root div#Correct. main.jsx is the entry script\: it imports the top component and renders it into the div with id root.
~<code>index.html</code>, which lists every component that the page will render#Not quite. index.html holds the empty root div and loads main.jsx; it lists no components.
~<code>package.json</code>, which names the component the app starts from#Not quite. package.json holds the dependencies and the dev and build scripts.
~<code>src/Shell.jsx</code> itself, which must still export the name App#Not quite. The file can export Shell; the file that imports it must use the new path.
}

::react-cf30-q03::[html]<p>A product card must show exactly one thing\: Sold out when <code>item.stock</code> is 0; otherwise <code>item.sale</code> when <code>item.onSale</code> is true; otherwise <code>item.price</code>.</p><p>Which return does that?</p>{
~<code>return &lt;p&gt;\{item.stock \=\=\= 0 &amp;&amp; 'Sold out'\}\{item.onSale ? item.sale \: item.price\}&lt;/p&gt;;</code>#Not quite. &amp;&amp; adds Sold out, but the ternary still runs, so a sold-out item shows a price as well.
~<code>return &lt;p&gt;\{item.stock &amp;&amp; (item.onSale ? item.sale \: item.price)\}&lt;/p&gt;;</code>#Not quite. With stock 0, &amp;&amp; renders the 0 itself, not Sold out.
~<code>return &lt;p&gt;\{item.stock \=\=\= 0 ? 'Sold out' \: item.onSale &amp;&amp; item.sale\}&lt;/p&gt;;</code>#Not quite. An item that is not on sale renders false, which shows nothing instead of its price.
=<code>return &lt;p&gt;\{item.stock \=\=\= 0 ? 'Sold out' \: item.onSale ? item.sale \: item.price\}&lt;/p&gt;;</code>#Correct. The first condition handles Sold out, and the second ternary picks one of the two prices, so exactly one thing shows.
}

::react-cf30-q04::[html]<p>A guest list component ends like this.</p><pre><code>const guests \= [
&nbsp; \{ id\: 'g1', name\: 'Ola', rsvp\: true \},
&nbsp; \{ id\: 'g2', name\: 'Ben', rsvp\: false \},
&nbsp; \{ id\: 'g3', name\: 'Ivy', rsvp\: true \},
];
const coming \= guests.filter(g \=&gt; g.rsvp);
return &lt;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;\{coming.length\} of \{guests.length\} coming&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;\{coming.map((\{ id, name \}) \=&gt; &lt;li key\=\{id\}&gt;\{name\}&lt;/li&gt;)\}&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/&gt;;</code></pre><p>What does the component show?</p>{
~3 of 3 coming, then Ola, Ben and Ivy#Not quite. filter keeps only the guests whose rsvp is true.
=2 of 3 coming, then Ola and Ivy#Correct. coming holds the two guests who replied yes, and map destructures each one to show its name.
~2 of 3 coming, then Ola, Ben and Ivy#Not quite. The list maps coming, not guests.
~2 of 3 coming, then g1 and g3#Not quite. The destructured name is shown; id is used only as the key.
}

::react-cf30-q05::[html]<p>Tracks arrive without ids, so the playlist renders <code>tracks.map(t \=&gt; &lt;Track key\=\{crypto.randomUUID()\} track\=\{t\} /&gt;)</code>. Each Track keeps its own liked state. Liking works, but the heart clears whenever the list renders again for any reason.</p><p>What is the cause and the fix?</p>{
=Keys change every render; add an id when a track is added#Correct. Keys must be stable\: create the id once, when the item is created, and store it with the item, not during render.
~Child state never survives a render; keep liked in the parent#Not quite. State survives renders as long as the key stays the same.
~randomUUID can repeat; use <code>Math.random()</code> for the key instead#Not quite. Both make a new key on every render.
~Track renders too often; wrap it in memo to keep its state#Not quite. memo is a performance tool, and a new key still means a new component.
}

::react-cf30-q06::[html]<p>Clicking the words Last name puts the cursor in the First name box, and <code>getByLabelText('Last name')</code> returns the first input.</p><pre><code>function Field(\{ label \}) \{
&nbsp; return &lt;&gt;&lt;label htmlFor\="field"&gt;\{label\}&lt;/label&gt;&lt;input id\="field" /&gt;&lt;/&gt;;
\}
&\#47;/ the form renders &lt;Field label\="First name" /&gt; and &lt;Field label\="Last name" /&gt;</code></pre><p>What is the fix?</p>{
~Wrap each label's text in a strong element#Not quite. Styling does not change which input a label points at.
~Write <code>for</code> instead of htmlFor, as in plain HTML#Not quite. JSX uses htmlFor; the problem is that both inputs share one id.
=Give each Field its own id for htmlFor and id#Correct. An id must be unique on the page; both labels point at the first element with id field.
~Add a placeholder to each input that names the field#Not quite. A placeholder is a hint, not a label, and the labels would still point at the same id.
}

::react-cf30-q07::[html]<p>OrdersPage renders OrdersTable and, beside it, Toolbar. Both need the selected order id, and nothing else uses it. A teammate proposes a new SelectionContext for it.</p><p>Which design fits the course's rule?</p>{
~Keep a copy in each component and sync them with an Effect#Not quite. Two copies can disagree; keep one source of truth.
~Create the context, since any shared value belongs in one#Not quite. Two siblings one level down are served by their parent; props are simpler here.
~Keep it in a module-level variable that both import#Not quite. Changing a plain variable does not re-render anything.
=Hold it in OrdersPage state and pass it down as props#Correct. Lift shared state to the closest common parent; context suits data read widely at many depths.
}

::react-cf30-q08::[html]<p>Signing up with a used address, the network tab shows a 400 whose body is <code>\{"errors"\: \{"emailAddress"\: "Already in use"\}\}</code>. No message appears by the field.</p><pre><code>if (res.status \=\=\= 400) \{
&nbsp; const body \= await res.json();
&nbsp; setErrors(body.errors);
\}
&\#47;/ next to the Email field\:
\{errors.email &amp;&amp; &lt;p id\="email-error"&gt;\{errors.email\}&lt;/p&gt;\}</code></pre><p>What is wrong?</p>{
~A 400 needs a form-level message, not a field message#Not quite. A 400 with per-field messages belongs next to each field.
=The API names the field emailAddress, not email#Correct. The API contract decides the shape; show each message by the field the API names.
~res.json() cannot read the body of a 400 response#Not quite. A 400 still has a body that json() can read.
~Field errors must be set before the request is sent#Not quite. Server validation arrives with the response.
}

::react-cf30-q09::[html]<p>The server fails with a 500, and the response body is an HTML error page.</p><pre><code>async function loadReport() \{
&nbsp; try \{
&nbsp;   const res \= await fetch('/api/report');
&nbsp;   const data \= await res.json();
&nbsp;   if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Server said ' + res.status);
&nbsp;   return data;
&nbsp; \} catch (e) \{
&nbsp;   showError(e.message);
&nbsp; \}
\}</code></pre><p>What message does showError receive?</p>{
~Server said 500, from the status check on res.ok#Not quite. res.json() runs first and throws on the HTML body, so the status check is never reached.
~Nothing, because a 500 response never reaches catch#Not quite. An error thrown anywhere in the try block, including the parsing error, lands in catch.
~The HTML page itself, as the returned data#Not quite. json() cannot parse HTML, so it throws.
=A parsing error from res.json()#Correct. The 500 resolves normally, then parsing the HTML body throws before the status check runs. Check res.ok before reading the body.
}

::react-cf30-q10::[html]<p>Basket owns <code>qty</code> in state and renders <code>&lt;Stepper qty\=\{qty\} onIncrease\=\{() \=&gt; qty + 1\} /&gt;</code>. Stepper's + button has <code>onClick\=\{onIncrease\}</code>. Clicking + changes neither Stepper's number nor Basket's total.</p><p>Why?</p>{
=The handler only computes qty + 1 and never sets state#Correct. Pass onIncrease\=\{() \=&gt; setQty(qty + 1)\}, so the click updates the parent's state.
~Stepper must call <code>onIncrease()</code> while rendering#Not quite. That would run it on every render instead of on click, and it still sets nothing.
~A child cannot trigger a change in its parent's state#Not quite. It can, through a handler prop that the parent passes down.
~Stepper must change its qty prop so Basket can read it#Not quite. A component must not change its props; the parent owns qty.
}

::react-cf30-q11::[html]<p>reserveSeat sends a request that takes about a second. From 0, the user clicks Reserve twice quickly, and both requests succeed.</p><pre><code>const [seats, setSeats] \= useState(0);
async function reserve() \{
&nbsp; await reserveSeat();
&nbsp; setSeats(seats + 1);
\}
return &lt;button onClick\=\{reserve\}&gt;Reserve (\{seats\})&lt;/button&gt;;</code></pre><p>What does the button show at the end, and what fixes it?</p>{
~Reserve (2), since each request finishes on its own#Not quite. Each handler reads seats from the render that created it, and that was 0 for both.
~Reserve (1); keep seats in a plain variable instead#Not quite. Changing a plain variable does not re-render, so the button would never update.
=Reserve (1); use <code>setSeats(s \=&gt; s + 1)</code> instead#Correct. Both handlers come from the same render, where seats is 0, so both set 1; an updater builds on the latest value.
~Reserve (0), because state cannot be set after await#Not quite. Setters work after await; both calls set 1.
}

::react-cf30-q12::[html]<p>The parent route is <code>&lt;Route path\="/shop" element\=\{&lt;Shop /&gt;\}&gt;</code>, and Shop renders a heading and an <code>&lt;Outlet /&gt;</code>. Cart should appear inside Shop at /shop/cart.</p><p>Which child route does that?</p>{
~<code>&lt;Route path\="/cart" element\=\{&lt;Cart /&gt;\} /&gt;</code>#Not quite. A child's path is relative; an absolute /cart cannot sit under /shop, and React Router reports an error.
=<code>&lt;Route path\="cart" element\=\{&lt;Cart /&gt;\} /&gt;</code>#Correct. A child's path is relative to its parent, with no leading slash.
~<code>&lt;Route path\="shop/cart" element\=\{&lt;Cart /&gt;\} /&gt;</code>#Not quite. Relative to /shop, this matches /shop/shop/cart.
~<code>&lt;Route index element\=\{&lt;Cart /&gt;\} /&gt;</code>#Not quite. An index route renders at /shop itself.
}

::react-cf30-q13::[html]<p>The user replaces the address in the field with ana@new.test and clicks Save.</p><pre><code>const [email, setEmail] \= useState('ana@old.test');
function submit(e) \{ e.preventDefault(); save(\{ email \}); \}
return &lt;form onSubmit\=\{submit\}&gt;&lt;input aria-label\="Email" defaultValue\=\{email\} /&gt;&lt;button&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/form&gt;;</code></pre><p>What does save receive?</p>{
~<code>\{ email\: 'ana@new.test' \}</code>, the text shown in the field#Not quite. The field is uncontrolled\: what the user types stays in the input and never reaches email state.
~Nothing, because submitting the form reloads the page first#Not quite. preventDefault stops the reload, so submit runs.
~<code>\{ email\: '' \}</code>, because typing cleared the starting value#Not quite. Typing changes only the input's own text; email state still holds the starting address.
=<code>\{ email\: 'ana@old.test' \}</code>, since nothing updates email state#Correct. defaultValue only gives the field its starting text; submit reads state, which nothing changed. Use value with onChange.
}

::react-cf30-q14::[html]<p><code>usePlaylist()</code> keeps tracks in <code>useState</code> and loads them in an Effect. NowPlaying and Queue both call it, and removing a track in Queue does not change NowPlaying.</p><p>Which change makes both components see the same tracks?</p>{
=Call usePlaylist once in a provider; read it with useContext#Correct. Each Hook call has its own state; one call in a common provider gives both components the same value.
~Give NowPlaying and Queue the same key prop#Not quite. A key identifies an element among its siblings; it never shares state.
~Rename the Hook so that it no longer starts with use#Not quite. The name only tells React that the Hook rules apply; each call still has its own state.
~Export the tracks array as a variable from the Hook's file#Not quite. A module variable is shared, but changing it does not re-render anything.
}

::react-cf30-q15::[html]<p>A search request fails with a network error, so status becomes 'error' and items stays [].</p><pre><code>if (status \=\=\= 'loading') return &lt;Spinner /&gt;;
if (items.length \=\=\= 0) return &lt;p&gt;No books match.&lt;/p&gt;;
if (status \=\=\= 'error') return &lt;p&gt;Could not load books.&lt;/p&gt;;
return &lt;BookList items\=\{items\} /&gt;;</code></pre><p>What does the user see, and what is the fix?</p>{
~Could not load books, since error is checked after loading#Not quite. The empty check comes first and already returns.
~The spinner, because a failed request stays in loading#Not quite. status is 'error', so the first check is false.
=No books match; check for the error before the empty case#Correct. Each state needs its own screen, and an empty result is not an error; here the empty check catches the failure first.
~An empty BookList, because items is still an array#Not quite. The empty check returns before BookList is reached.
}

::react-cf30-q16::[html]<p>In development the timer counts two seconds for every real second; in the production build it counts correctly.</p><pre><code>function Timer() \{
&nbsp; const [secs, setSecs] \= useState(0);
&nbsp; useEffect(() \=&gt; \{
&nbsp;   setInterval(() \=&gt; setSecs(s \=&gt; s + 1), 1000);
&nbsp; \}, []);
&nbsp; return &lt;p&gt;\{secs\} s&lt;/p&gt;;
\}</code></pre><p>What is the cause and the fix?</p>{
~The updater adds twice; use <code>setSecs(secs + 1)</code> instead#Not quite. Inside this Effect secs stays 0, so that would show 1 for ever.
=Two intervals run; return a cleanup that clears the interval#Correct. In development Strict Mode mounts, cleans up and mounts again to expose a missing cleanup, so two intervals tick. Keep the id, const id \= setInterval(...), and return () \=&gt; clearInterval(id).
~The empty array makes the Effect run on every render#Not quite. [] runs it after mount only.
~Development runs timers at double speed; nothing needs fixing#Not quite. Two intervals really run in development; the production build hides the missing cleanup.
}

::react-cf30-q17::[html]<p>The app runs at app.example.test, and the API's CORS setup allows credentials. Login returns 200 and sets a session cookie, but /api/me returns 401.</p><pre><code>await fetch('https\://api.example.test/api/login', \{
&nbsp; method\: 'POST',
&nbsp; headers\: \{ 'Content-Type'\: 'application/json' \},
&nbsp; body\: JSON.stringify(form),
\});
const me \= await fetch('https\://api.example.test/api/me', \{ credentials\: 'include' \});</code></pre><p>What fixes it?</p>{
=Add <code>credentials\: 'include'</code> to the login request too#Correct. Without it, the browser does not store a cookie from a cross-origin response, so there is nothing to send later.
~Add an Authorization\: Bearer header to /api/me#Not quite. A Bearer header carries a JWT; this login uses a cookie.
~Remove <code>credentials\: 'include'</code> from /api/me#Not quite. Then the cookie would not be sent even once stored.
~Make the session cookie HttpOnly so fetch can send it#Not quite. HttpOnly hides the cookie from scripts; it does not affect storing or sending.
}

::react-cf30-q18::[html]<p>Ravi deletes <code>spring.profiles.active\=dev</code> from application.properties on the shared server and restarts the back end with the team's usual command, <code>SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE\=dev mvn spring-boot\:run</code>. Requests with no credentials still succeed.</p><p>Why?</p>{
~The deleted line is cached until the server is rebooted#Not quite. The application re-reads its configuration on each start.
~The front end still sends the dev profile in its requests#Not quite. The profile is a back-end setting; requests do not choose it.
~Turning off dev also needs a VITE_ variable in the front end#Not quite. VITE_ variables configure the front end, not Spring.
=The start command still sets dev; remove it there too#Correct. Remove dev wherever it was set\: the environment, a run command or a config file. Then check the startup log.
}

::react-cf30-q19::[html]<p>On /orders/7, the user clicks Next order.</p><pre><code>function OrderPage() \{
&nbsp; const \{ id \} \= useParams(); // route orders/\:id
&nbsp; return &lt;Link to\=\{'/orders/' + (id + 1)\}&gt;Next order&lt;/Link&gt;;
\}</code></pre><p>Where does the link go?</p>{
~/orders/8, because id + 1 adds one to the order number#Not quite. id is the string '7', so + joins text instead of adding.
~/orders/NaN, because a string cannot take part in +#Not quite. + with a string joins text, which gives '71'.
=/orders/71\: id is the string '7', so + joins the text#Correct. Route params are always strings; convert first, as in Number(id) + 1.
~/orders/7 again, because Link ignores a computed path#Not quite. Link navigates to whatever path string it is given.
}

::react-cf30-q20::[html]<p>The orders page shows its title as <code>&lt;div className\="title"&gt;Orders&lt;/div&gt;</code>, and it is on screen as soon as the page renders. The test line <code>screen.getByRole('heading', \{ name\: 'Orders' \})</code> throws.</p><p>What is the best fix?</p>{
~Await findByRole instead, because the title appears later#Not quite. The title is there at once; findBy would wait and still find no heading.
=Make the title a real heading, such as an h1#Correct. getByRole finds elements by their role, and a div has no heading role. The fix is semantic markup, which also helps screen-reader users.
~Use queryByRole, which returns the element instead of throwing#Not quite. queryBy returns null here, because no element has the heading role.
~Query by the class name title, which is more reliable#Not quite. Tests that query by class break when styling changes; give the page the right role instead.
}

::react-cf30-q21::[html]<p>Save has <code>disabled\=\{saving\}</code>, yet a quick double click still creates two notes.</p><pre><code>async function save() \{
&nbsp; const res \= await postNote(draft);
&nbsp; setSaving(true);
&nbsp; if (res.ok) \{ setNotes([...notes, await res.json()]); setDraft(''); \}
&nbsp; setSaving(false);
\}</code></pre><p>What is the cause?</p>{
~setSaving(false) re-enables the button too early; remove that line#Not quite. The button must be enabled again once the request ends; the bug is when saving is set.
~setDraft('') runs too late; clear the draft before sending#Not quite. Clear the typed text only after success, so a failure keeps it.
~The res.ok check must come before setSaving(true)#Not quite. Both lines run after the request has already been sent twice.
=saving turns on only after the request; set it before postNote#Correct. The button must be disabled while the request is in flight, so set saving just before sending, and clear it when the request ends.
}

::react-cf30-q22::[html]<p>Login sets an HttpOnly session cookie. The app also keeps <code>\{ name, role \}</code> in localStorage, and RequireRole admits /payroll when that stored role is MANAGER. The server allows /api/payroll only for MANAGER. Kim, a clerk, edits the stored role to MANAGER and opens /payroll, which loads /api/payroll.</p><p>What happens?</p>{
=The page opens, but /api/payroll answers 403#Correct. The guard trusts what the browser stores, so the page opens; the server checks the real role behind Kim's session and refuses the data.
~The guard blocks her; her session cookie says clerk#Not quite. The guard reads localStorage; scripts cannot read an HttpOnly cookie.
~She sees every salary; the app treats her as manager#Not quite. Only the interface changed; the server decides what data she gets.
~The API answers 401 and signs her out#Not quite. Her session is valid, so she is authenticated; refusing a valid user is a 403.
}

::react-cf30-q23::[html]<p>/notes lists NotesContext's notes. A delete on the detail page succeeds and removes the row there, but /notes still lists it.</p><pre><code>const \{ notes \} \= useContext(NotesContext);
const [list, setList] \= useState(notes);
async function remove(id) \{
&nbsp; const res \= await fetch('/api/notes/' + id, \{ method\: 'DELETE' \});
&nbsp; if (res.ok) setList(list.filter(n \=&gt; n.id !\=\= id));
\}</code></pre><p>What is wrong?</p>{
~Re-send the DELETE request from /notes#Not quite. The server already deleted it; the shared list in the provider was never changed.
~The filter compares ids of mismatched types#Not quite. The row disappears on the detail page, so the filter works.
=setList filtered a copy, not the context's#Correct. useState(notes) copies the list once; the provider's state, which /notes reads, is untouched. Call a setter the provider passes down.
~/notes must reload the page first#Not quite. A reload would refetch, but the real bug is updating a private copy.
}

::react-cf30-q24::[html]<p>The parent holds tags state (<code>['red']</code>) and a search box with its own state, and renders <code>&lt;TagPicker tags\=\{tags\} onChange\=\{setTags\} /&gt;</code>. Add blue does nothing; typing in the search then shows blue.</p><pre><code>function TagPicker(\{ tags, onChange \}) \{
&nbsp; function add(tag) \{ tags.push(tag); onChange(tags); \}
&nbsp; return &lt;&gt;&lt;p&gt;\{tags.join(', ')\}&lt;/p&gt;&lt;button onClick\=\{() \=&gt; add('blue')\}&gt;Add blue&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/&gt;;
\}</code></pre><p>Why does blue appear only then?</p>{
~setTags applies only after the next event#Not quite. It runs at once, but with the same array React sees no change.
=The re-render showed the already mutated array#Correct. push changed the state array in place, and setTags with the same array caused no render; the next render, from the search box, shows the mutation. Send a new array\: onChange([...tags, tag]).
~The search box and tags share one state#Not quite. They are separate state variables; the search update only re-rendered the parent.
~Typing reset the tags back to their initial value#Not quite. useState keeps its value across renders; the array was changed in place.
}

::react-cf30-q25::[html]<p>After sign-in calls setUser, the pages greet Lee, but NavBar still shows Sign in. NavBar reads <code>useContext(AuthContext).user</code>; the context was made with <code>createContext(\{ user\: null \})</code>.</p><pre><code>function App() \{
&nbsp; const [user, setUser] \= useState(null);
&nbsp; return &lt;&gt;&lt;NavBar /&gt;&lt;AuthContext value\=\{\{ user, setUser \}\}&gt;&lt;Pages /&gt;&lt;/AuthContext&gt;&lt;/&gt;;
\}</code></pre><p>Why is only NavBar stale?</p>{
=NavBar sits outside the provider and reads the default#Correct. useContext uses the nearest provider above the component, or else the default. Move NavBar inside AuthContext.
~setUser never re-renders the components that read a context#Not quite. The pages updated, so the provided value did change.
~NavBar needs <code>memo</code> before it can re-render#Not quite. memo only skips renders; it never adds them.
~The provided object must be mutated, not replaced#Not quite. Replacing the value is right; NavBar simply cannot see this provider.
}

::react-cf30-q26::[html]<p>teams holds <code>\{ id, lead\: \{ name \} \}</code> objects; Undo restores the last history snapshot. A lead is renamed from Ana to Bo, then Undo is clicked.</p><pre><code>function renameLead(id, name) \{
&nbsp; setHistory([...history, teams]);
&nbsp; teams.find(t \=&gt; t.id \=\=\= id).lead.name \= name;
&nbsp; setTeams(teams.map(t \=&gt; (t.id \=\=\= id ? \{ ...t \} \: t)));
\}</code></pre><p>Which name shows after Undo?</p>{
~Ana; the snapshot was saved first#Not quite. The snapshot holds the same lead objects as state, and the rename changed one of them in place.
~Undo throws, since history holds old objects#Not quite. Nothing throws; the old array simply shares the changed lead.
~Ana; map returned a new array and team#Not quite. The array and team are new, but lead is the same object in both.
=Bo; the snapshot shares the renamed lead#Correct. The team was copied, but lead was mutated, and the snapshot shares that lead. Copy it too\: lead\: \{ ...t.lead, name \}.
}

::react-cf30-q27::[html]<p>AuthContext's user is null until /api/me answers. On a fresh load, the app crashes with\: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'role').</p><pre><code>function Toolbar() \{
&nbsp; const \{ user \} \= useContext(AuthContext);
&nbsp; return &lt;nav&gt;&lt;Link to\="/orders"&gt;Orders&lt;/Link&gt;\{user.role \=\=\= 'ADMIN' &amp;&amp; &lt;button&gt;Delete all&lt;/button&gt;\}&lt;/nav&gt;;
\}</code></pre><p>Which replacement for the button line fixes it?</p>{
~<code>\{user.role \=\=\= 'ADMIN' ? &lt;button&gt;Delete all&lt;/button&gt; \: null\}</code>#Not quite. It still reads user.role while user is null.
~<code>\{user &amp;&amp; &lt;button&gt;Delete all&lt;/button&gt;\}</code>#Not quite. That shows the button to every signed-in user, not only admins.
=<code>\{user &amp;&amp; user.role \=\=\= 'ADMIN' &amp;&amp; &lt;button&gt;Delete all&lt;/button&gt;\}</code>#Correct. While user is null, &amp;&amp; stops at the first operand, so user.role is never read and nothing renders; once the user loads, only admins see the button.
~<code>\{user.role &amp;&amp; user.role \=\=\= 'ADMIN' &amp;&amp; &lt;button&gt;Delete all&lt;/button&gt;\}</code>#Not quite. The first check already reads user.role while user is null.
}

::react-cf30-q28::[html]<p>After Sam's session expires, the dashboard greets him with Hello, undefined. The API answered 401 with <code>\{"error"\: "unauthorized"\}</code>.</p><pre><code>useEffect(() \=&gt; \{
&nbsp; fetch('/api/me', \{ credentials\: 'include' \})
&nbsp;   .then(res \=&gt; res.json()).then(setUser);
\}, []);
&\#47;/ renders &lt;h1&gt;\{user ? 'Hello, ' + user.name \: 'Loading...'\}&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre><p>What is the cause?</p>{
~Add <code>[user]</code> so the Effect reruns when sessions end#Not quite. That reruns the request whenever user changes and still stores the error body.
=The 401 body was stored as user; check res.ok first#Correct. fetch rejects only when the request itself fails; a 401 resolves, so check the status, and on 401 clear the user and go to sign-in.
~The cookie was dropped; add an Authorization header#Not quite. The request reached the API; the bug is treating its 401 body as the user.
~fetch rejected on 401; add a catch for guests#Not quite. A 401 resolves; nothing rejected.
}

::react-cf30-q29::[html]<p>/orders sits in RequireUser, which reads user from AuthContext and renders <code>&lt;Navigate to\="/sign-in" replace /&gt;</code> if it is null. Lee's login succeeds, yet Lee lands on /sign-in again. SignIn has\:</p><pre><code>const [user, setUser] \= useState(null);
const go \= useNavigate();
async function submit(form) \{
&nbsp; const res \= await login(form);
&nbsp; if (res.ok) \{ setUser(await res.json()); go('/orders'); \}
\}</code></pre><p>Why?</p>{
=The user went into SignIn's state, not AuthContext#Correct. Each component's state is its own; keep the signed-in user in the state behind the provider, and set that.
~go('/orders') needs replace\: true to pass the guard#Not quite. replace only changes history; the guard checks the user.
~The cookie is set only after a reload#Not quite. The guard reads app state, not the cookie.
~RequireUser must call login first#Not quite. The guard only reads who is signed in; SignIn does the login.
}

::react-cf30-q30::[html]<p>Signed out, Lee is on /home and clicks a Link to /orders. Lee signs in, lands on /orders, and then presses Back once.</p><pre><code>// RequireUser, while user is null\:
return &lt;Navigate to\="/sign-in" replace /&gt;;
&\#47;/ SignIn, once the login succeeds\:
navigate('/orders', \{ replace\: true \});</code></pre><p>Where does Lee land?</p>{
~/sign-in, the entry left by the guard's redirect#Not quite. The guard replaced /orders with /sign-in, and the sign-in replaced that with /orders.
~/orders again, because the guard repeats its redirect#Not quite. Back moves to the previous history entry, which is /home.
~The page before /home, since Back skips replaced entries#Not quite. replace swaps one entry; /home is still the entry before /orders.
=/home, because both redirects replaced their entry#Correct. Navigate with replace swapped /orders for /sign-in, and the sign-in swapped that for /orders, so /home is one step back.
}
